>
  <p class="note">Your signed management authority is a binding contract. Check its conditions before ending or transferring management, and obtain professional advice if your circumstances require it.</p>

  <h2>Start with the actual problem</h2>
  <p>“I am unhappy with my property manager” can mean very different things. Identify what is driving the review: slow communication, unresolved maintenance, weak reporting, uncertainty around arrears, leasing concerns, repeated staff changes or simply not knowing who is accountable.</p>
  <p>The clearer the problem, the easier it is to evaluate whether a new manager's operating model actually addresses it.</p>

  <h2>Read the current authority before making promises or dates</h2>
  <p>Consumer Affairs Victoria explains that engaging an agent involves an exclusive leasing and/or managing authority. It also advises rental providers to check the conditions of that authority before ending the property-management contract.</p>
  <p>That is why a sensible switching conversation starts with the current agreement rather than an assumption that every transfer works the same way.</p>

  <h2>Questions to ask the next manager</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Who will actually manage my property after I sign?</li>
    <li>How are urgent and non-urgent maintenance decisions communicated and approved?</li>
    <li>What should I expect from routine-inspection reporting and follow-through?</li>
    <li>How do you monitor rent, arrears, lease milestones and renewal timing?</li>
    <li>When something is going wrong, who owns the next action?</li>
    <li>What fees, expenses and authorities will be documented in the management agreement?</li>
    <li>What information and documents do you need for an orderly handover?</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What an orderly handover may need</h2>
  <p>The exact transfer depends on the property and existing arrangements, but useful handover planning commonly considers keys, rental and management documents, renter contact details, condition and inspection records, maintenance history, financial records and communication with the renter about the new arrangements.</p>
  <p>Consumer Affairs Victoria notes that keys and relevant documents need to be collected or transferred and that the renter must be formally advised of new management arrangements.</p>

  <h2>Do not switch only for a nicer promise</h2>
  <p>Most agencies can promise communication, care and service. Ask how those promises show up operationally: who contacts you, what gets documented, how issues are escalated and how you know something has been completed.</p>
  <p>A better working relationship should be visible in the process, not only in the sales conversation.</p>
